{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you shall present a burnt offering, a fire offering, a pleasing odor to YHWH: thirteen young bulls.</b> The offerings of the festival mentioned in this passage number seventy, corresponding to the seventy nations. They would offer them on the first day thirteen, on the second day twelve, on the third day eleven, and so they all diminish progressively. This was a sign of the annihilation of the seventy idolatrous nations, that they would all perish from the world. And you already know that the seventy ancient nations had seventy princes on high, for every nation has a prince appointed over it and governing it, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 10:20) ״ושר מלכות פרס וגו״, and thereafter (ibid.) ״והנה שר יון בא״. And you will not find in this entire passage across all seven days that it mentions לה' except on the first day alone. The reason is that Israel bestows benefit upon all those forces, because the goods of the world come through them, so that the forces may be unified one with another, and each and every one gives acknowledgment to Him from whom it receives power — through the uppermost Cause. Thus they all recognize and acknowledge that a singular Master rules over them. Therefore He mentioned here לה' on the first day alone, to teach you that the intention in all seventy bulls should be לה' alone, for He is the head of all dominions and all configurations. This is what they said, of blessed memory, in tractate Sukkah: Woe to the nations of the world, who lost something and do not know what they lost. When the Temple stood, the altar would atone for them; now, who atones for them? They thereby taught us that Israel would offer, during the seven days of Sukkot, seventy offerings to atone for the seventy nations. And the offering was to bring the forces near to one another, so that the power of each and every nation would recognize the force that is above it, and so from force to force, and from the upper to the upper, and from the high to the highest, up to the first Cause, who is supreme over all. They all acknowledge and unify that they have no greatness or sovereignty except from Him, and He lacks nothing. This is the complete unification. And this is the meaning of the term קרבן — a drawing near of the forces, for they all acknowledge Him from whom they receive power." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> והקרבתם אשה — this is what the verse says (Psalms 109:4): ״תחת אהבתי ישטנוני ואני תפלה״. You find that at the festival Israel offers seventy bulls on behalf of the seventy nations of the world; therefore they were obligated to love us — yet not only do they not love us, but they hate us, as it says: ״תחת אהבתי ישטנוני ואני תפלה״. And they further expounded on the offerings of the festival: the Torah teaches proper conduct through these offerings — that if a person is hosted by his fellow, on the first day he receives him well and feeds him poultry; the next day he feeds him fish; the next day he feeds him vegetables, and so he diminishes progressively until he feeds him legumes."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:55.410887703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}